        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the Los Angeles market",
        "market_text": "<p>Los Angeles buyers are as image-literate as any in the country — they scroll past anything that doesn't look like the homes they see on Instagram and Zillow's front page. An empty Spanish bungalow or a bare new build in the hills leaves the lifestyle to the imagination, and in LA the lifestyle is the product.</p><p>Indoor-outdoor space is where LA listings win: staged patios, decks and yards routinely out-click staged living rooms. Our home staging covers exteriors too, and our day-to-dusk editing turns a flat afternoon exterior into the golden-hour shot that anchors the listing.</p><p>Los Angeles is also a market where physical staging is genuinely expensive and genuinely slow — coastal and hillside listings can run thousands a month, and scheduling a crew up a canyon road is its own problem. Staging the photos removes the logistics and fixes the cost at {price:virtual-staging} a photo, whether the listing closes in ten days or ninety.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Staged for the LA buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>From Silver Lake creative contemporaries to Westside family traditionals, our designers furnish to the buyer each pocket attracts. Every image is MLS-ready, disclosed as virtually staged, and delivered in 24 to 48 hours.</p><p>Architectural listings get handled as architecture. A Spanish revival, a mid-century post-and-beam and a new spec build each want a different furniture vocabulary, and staging that ignores the house tends to read as a stock room dropped into someone's photo.</p>",
